/* ============================================================================
poisson-approximation.epher -- the binomial for rare events as a Poisson

Approximates the binomial probability P(X <= 3) for X with num = 1000
trials and p = 0.002 (a rare event) by the Poisson with rate
lambda = num * p = 2, using the builtin poissoncdf (guide 1.20). The
rule of thumb: the Poisson approximates the binomial well when num is
large and p small, here with num * p = 2. Both answers agree to about
two ten-thousandths.

Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
Everything also works line by line in the TUI.

The knobs to edit are `num`, `p` and `kk` below. Everything above the
results section is machinery you can leave alone.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the knobs -----------------------------------------------------------------
const num = 1000   // trials, say components tested
const p = 0.002  // failure probability per trial
const kk = 3      // the cut point: P(X <= kk)

// ---- the exact binomial and the Poisson approximation ---------------------------------
print("exact binomial P(X <= 3):", binomcdf(kk, num, p))
print("Poisson approximation:", poissoncdf(kk, num * p))
print("difference:", binomcdf(kk, num, p) - poissoncdf(kk, num * p))

// ---- the parameters --------------------------------------------------------------------
print("rate lambda = num * p:", num * p)

/* ---- expected output ----
= exact binomial P(X <= 3): 0.857304238915
= Poisson approximation: 0.857123460509
= difference: 0.000180778405915
= rate lambda = num * p: 2
*/
